# A Coxeter group, the engine of every hyperbolic tiling, modeled by the kernel. A Coxeter group is generated by
# REFLECTIONS: each generator s squares to the identity, and a pair of generators satisfies a braid relation
# (s t)^m = identity. The example is the symmetric group on three letters, A2 = S3, the symmetry of the triangle. Its two
# generators are the adjacent reflections (swap the first two points, swap the last two), acting on three points. The
# kernel proves each reflection is an INVOLUTION (s^2 = identity) and the BRAID RELATION s1 s2 s1 = s2 s1 s2 (the order-3
# relation, m = 3), by induction over the three points. Coxeter groups classify the regular tilings: the spherical ones
# are finite, the Euclidean ones affine, and the HYPERBOLIC ones infinite, and the substrate's cell symmetry F4 is the
# Coxeter group of the 24-cell.

# Three points the reflections permute.
form point
  case p1
  case p2
  case p3

# The first reflection: swap points one and two, fix point three.
task s1
  take x, like point
  like point
  fork case, read x
    case p1
      send back
        make p2
    case p2
      send back
        make p1
    case p3
      send back
        make p3

# The second reflection: swap points two and three, fix point one.
task s2
  take x, like point
  like point
  fork case, read x
    case p1
      send back
        make p1
    case p2
      send back
        make p3
    case p3
      send back
        make p2

# s1 IS AN INVOLUTION: s1 (s1 x) = x. A reflection done twice is the identity, the defining property of a Coxeter
# generator.
rule s1-is-an-involution
  seat x, like point
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call s1
        call s1
          read x
      read x
  fold x

# s2 IS AN INVOLUTION: s2 (s2 x) = x.
rule s2-is-an-involution
  seat x, like point
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call s2
        call s2
          read x
      read x
  fold x

# THE BRAID RELATION: s1 s2 s1 = s2 s1 s2, the order-three Coxeter relation (s1 s2)^3 = identity written out. Both sides
# send each point the same way (they equal the reflection swapping points one and three). This single relation, with the
# two involutions, presents the whole group S3. Proved over all three points.
rule braid-relation-of-a-two
  seat x, like point
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call s1
        call s2
          call s1
            read x
      call s2
        call s1
          call s2
            read x
  fold x
